+
+## Abnormal Codex quota resets
+
+Each shared Codex runtime uses `account/rateLimits/updated` only to request a fresh `account/rateLimits/read` snapshot. The detector compares account reads for the same account and metered limit, rebuilding its baseline when the account changes. It identifies the weekly and five-hour windows by their durations (10,080 and 300 minutes), regardless of their primary/secondary positions. An abnormal reset requires weekly remaining quota to cross from at most 90% to strictly above 90%, increase by at least one percentage point, and do so before the previously announced weekly reset time. The five-hour window must also have strictly more than 90% remaining; a missing window does not confirm a reset. Ordinary quota consumption, a 31% to 32% reporting fluctuation, exactly 90% remaining, natural rollover and repeated snapshots do not trigger the policy. When a reset is confirmed, HAPI updates every loaded session owned by that runtime: `ultra` reasoning becomes `xhigh`, and the fast service tier becomes standard. Both changes use one native settings update.
+
+The policy acts on sessions currently managed by the shared runtime. It does not resume dormant native threads solely to rewrite their settings; their next explicit settings choice remains authoritative.
 
 Android and iOS continue to use the deployed website for Web UI changes, so normal Web releases reach the app without rebuilding an APK or IPA. The `mobile` component validates and synchronizes native projects when native bridge code changes; installable signed artifacts remain the responsibility of the existing Capacitor CI workflow and are not recorded as installed by this command.
